  there since the earliest days of their  history  even as late as a second age however the  land looks very different to how it's  portrayed in the lord of the rings  for one thing galadriel and her husband  keller born aren't in charge  at least not initially part way through  the second age galadriel does arrive in  loflorien  but even then as a visitor and not a  ruler the huge silverbacked and  golden-leafed malone trees that define  the look of the third ages lothlorien  aren't there either  as galadriel is the one who initially  brings the seeds for those mighty  arboreal wonders  nonetheless lothlorien plays an  important role in the events of the  second age  the realm is ruled by the elvish leader  named amdia who fights and dies  alongside his kin  during the war of the last alliance so  while this version of lovelorian will  likely have a very different vibe to  galadriel's realm  you can probably make a safe bet that  it'll appear at least once in amazon's  upcoming series  the fellowship's journey to the mines of  moria is one of the most famous  sequences in the lord of the rings  the nine travelers expect to meet  gimli's cousin barlin  but their hopes are quickly dashed when  they discover the dwarven kingdom in  ruins  maurya is also notorious for being home  to durian's bane  the monstrous balrog that gandalf  confronts on the bridge of kaza doom  you know what they awoke in the darkness  of hazard  shadow and flame  in the second age however there's no  fiery demon on the premises just yet  as the dwarven kingdom of kaza doom is  still very much in its heyday  as such during the time of amazon series  all those md hallways and dusty tombs  will be well lit  thriving and positively filled with  dwarves the doors of doom were also  constructed during the second age  that's a kind of collaboration between  the dwarves of kaza doom  and the elvish craftsman of aragian  chief miss keller brimble  the elf prince who forged the three  rings fangorn fore